{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"3058","artifact_type":"feature-context","name":"plan/feature-context-bounded-plan-task-artifact-reads.md@sha256:a606de1de8d3baa60f304dd7b09b3763462475defcc0b298a149e6b231f8a08d"},"owner_reference":"3058","content":"# ARTIFACT:DISCOVERY\n\nRevision 2, 2026-09-20. Wave 0 research corrected two statements in revision 1: there are five unbounded surfaces, not four, and `backlog_get_ready_sam_tasks` does not embed task bodies. Both corrections are verified below.\n\n## Feature\n\nBounded responses for plan reads, task reads, artifact content reads, and ready-task listings.\n\n## Problem Statement\n\nFive read surfaces return stored content with no size guard. A harness commonly caps a single tool response near 10,000 tokens. When a response exceeds that cap the call fails outright rather than degrading, and the agent gets nothing \u2014 not a smaller view, not an error it can act on.\n\nThe five surfaces, each confirmed on `7e6381259`:\n\n| Surface | Location | What is unbounded |\n|---|---|---|\n| `sam_plan(action=\"read\")` | `sam_schema/server.py:95` | The whole plan record |\n| `sam_task(action=\"read\")` | `sam_schema/server.py:167` | The whole task record |\n| `sam_plan(action=\"ready\")` | `sam_schema/server_plan_ops.py:233` | Returns `ReadyTasksResult.ready_tasks: list[Task]` \u2014 every ready task's full record, every markdown field included |\n| `artifact_read` | `backlog_core/server.py:3522` | Raw markdown content, no parsing or navigation |\n| `backlog_get_ready_sam_tasks` | `backlog_core/server.py:3606` | Task count only. Each entry is already trimmed to `id`, `name`, `agent`, `skills`, `issue_number` at `backlog_core/operations.py:5107-5146` |\n\nThe two \"ready\" surfaces are easy to conflate and behave differently. `backlog_get_ready_sam_tasks` trims each entry and is unbounded only in how many entries it returns. `sam_plan(action=\"ready\")` returns full `Task` models and is bounded by neither count nor size.\n\n`sam_schema/` imports `progressive_markdown` nowhere. It has no token-budget constant.\n\nExisting plan and task documentation is silent on content size. `sam_schema/core/models.py:135` (`Task`) and `:279` (`Plan`) describe field structure only. The single repo hit for size language near plans, `skills/task-decomposition/SKILL.md:155`, is about task count during decomposition, not response size on a read.\n\n## Goals\n\n1. Each of the five surfaces returns a usable response regardless of how large the stored content is, or how many tasks are ready.\n2. When a response would exceed the budget, the caller receives a smaller form naming what exists and how to ask for a part of it, rather than a failed call.\n3. A caller can reach the whole of an over-budget response across successive calls, with nothing dropped.\n4. A ready-task listing reports what is ready without its size growing without limit \u2014 by count on one surface, by count and body size on the other.\n5. An agent reading the plan and task documentation learns that large records exist and how to read one.\n\n## Anti-Goals\n\n1. The default item-read path. That is #3057.\n2. Pagination of `backlog_view(map=True)`. That is #3059.\n3. Changing what a plan, task, or artifact stores. This is delivery, not content.\n4. Raising or removing the harness cap.\n5. Any change to `backlog_view` or `backlog_list`, which already bound their own responses.\n\n## Requirements\n\n### Functional\n\n1. No response from the five surfaces exceeds the configured token budget, whatever the stored content's size or the ready-task count.\n2. An over-budget response is replaced automatically with a smaller form. No caller opt-in, and no prior knowledge of the size.\n3. The smaller form names the exact call that retrieves a specific part.\n4. Every addressable part of an over-budget response is reachable across calls. Nothing is dropped, elided, or truncated away.\n5. `sam_plan(action=\"ready\")` stops returning every ready task's full record. Either it trims per entry the way `backlog_get_ready_sam_tasks` already does, or it pages over full bodies.\n6. Both ready-task surfaces bound their entry count.\n7. A caller may request a smaller response than the configured default.\n8. The plan and task documentation states that large records exist and describes how to read one.\n\n### Non-Functional\n\n1. The budget is the engine's single constant, `_DEFAULT_BUDGET` at `progressive_markdown/models.py:15`, resolving to `MAX_MCP_OUTPUT_TOKENS - 500` and falling back to 9,500. It must not exceed the harness cap.\n2. Token counts use the `cl100k_base` encoding, matching every other budget in this plugin.\n3. Existing callers keep working. A response that already fits the budget is unchanged. The consumer set is wide and includes this plugin's own agents and skills, so a shape change edits prompt contracts, not only client code.\n4. Each tool's output schema stays within its per-tool ceiling, and the total stays within the 13,000-token ceiling in `tests/test_tool_output_schemas.py`. Measured headroom on that total is 227 tokens.\n\n## Design Questions for Planning\n\n1. The acceptance criteria say these reads should \"route through the markdown engine\". Wave 0 established that this cannot be one instruction. `ProgressiveMarkdownNavigator` (`progressive_markdown/navigator.py:29`) addresses a heading tree parsed from markdown text. `Task` and `Plan` are flat Pydantic records whose individual fields hold markdown, with no heading tree for the navigator to walk. Decide per surface: `artifact_read` is a direct navigator fit; the two ready listings fit `progressive_markdown/list_navigator.py`'s `ProgressiveDisclosure`, which is a collection engine rather than a document one; `sam_plan(read)` and `sam_task(read)` fit neither today.\n2. For a typed record, decide whether bounding happens per markdown-valued field, or by rendering the record to markdown and navigating that. This is the central design decision and no existing code answers it.\n3. What the compact form contains for a plan, a task, an artifact, and each listing. R3's definition at `docs/agent-markdown-consumption-contract.md:105-111` is item-shaped and names item metadata, top description and artifact inventory. It does not describe a task or plan shape, so `backlog_view`'s compact manifest is precedent for what a compact form is for, not a template to copy.\n4. Address grammar for navigating into a large record. `backlog_core` uses dot ordinals; the engine's renderer emits a different selector grammar. #3059 established that mixing them breaks callers.\n5. Whether the smaller-page parameter matches #3059's choice, `page_budget` in tokens.\n6. Whether to migrate `sam_plan(action=\"list\")`'s `_paginate_results` (`sam_schema/server_plan_ops.py:131`) to the engine's `paginate_results` (`progressive_markdown/list_navigator.py:539`) as part of this work. The engine function documents itself as a drop-in replacement for exactly that function, and the migration never happened. There are three independent token-budget implementations in this repo \u2014 `backlog_view`'s `_VIEW_TOKEN_BUDGET` path, `backlog_list`'s `_resolve_effective_limit`, and this one \u2014 and none imports `progressive_markdown`. R1 forbids a second implementation regardless of whether it works.\n\n## References\n\n- `docs/agent-markdown-consumption-contract.md` \u2014 R1 through R8. R2 forbids dropping any addressable node to fit a budget. R3 defines the automatic compact form.\n- `docs/component-architecture.md:44-48`, `:74-81`, `:145-151` \u2014 package ownership, and the record that routing plan and task reads through the engine is tracked by this issue.\n- `progressive_markdown/navigator.py:29`, `progressive_markdown/list_navigator.py:539` \u2014 the document engine and the collection engine.\n- `sam_schema/core/models.py:135`, `:279`, `:599-614` \u2014 `Task`, `Plan`, `ReadyTasksResult`.\n- `tests/test_tool_output_schemas.py` \u2014 the output-schema ceilings.\n- Parent: #3054. Siblings: #3057, #3059, #3078.\n- PR #3551's description lists this issue as outstanding.\n\n## Resolved Questions\n\n| Question | Answer | Source |\n|----------|--------|--------|\n| Who consumes these surfaces? | This plugin's own agents and skills across the SAM pipeline, not only external clients. A shape change edits prompt contracts. | Wave 0 caller sweep |\n| What observable outcome is expected? | Every call returns something usable, and large content is reachable in full across calls. | Item acceptance criteria |\n| When does the behaviour trigger? | On every read, when the response would exceed the budget. No caller opt-in. | Contract R3 |\n| Why is this P0? | An unbounded response against a hard cap fails rather than degrades. | Item Context section |\n| Has any of this been implemented? | No. PR #3551 lists #3058 as still tracked. | `gh pr view 3551` |\n| Do the surfaces apply a budget today? | No. `sam_schema/` has no budget constant and imports `progressive_markdown` nowhere. | Verified on `7e6381259` |\n| Does plan or task documentation mention size? | No. The models' docstrings describe structure only. | `sam_schema/core/models.py:135`, `:279` |\n| Can the navigator address a typed record? | No. It addresses a markdown heading tree. `Task` and `Plan` are flat Pydantic records. | `progressive_markdown/navigator.py:29`, `indexer.py` |\n| Does `backlog_get_ready_sam_tasks` embed task bodies? | No. It trims to five fields per entry. Revision 1 of this artifact said otherwise and was wrong. | `backlog_core/operations.py:5107-5146` |\n\n## Open Questions\n\n- The boundary between this issue and #3078. Wave 0 found that `docs/component-architecture.md:145-151` attributes `artifact_read`'s lack of engine involvement to #3078, while this issue's own text claims artifact content reads. `artifact_read` is also the one surface that is a clean fit for the document navigator, so it is the most attractive piece to implement and the most likely to be built twice. Either #3078 is a subset of this issue, or one of the two drops artifact reads. The owner decides, before task decomposition.\n\n## User Confirmation\n\n- [x] Auto mode active. Treated as confirmed per the skill's AUTO_MODE gate; not confirmed by the user in conversation.\n"}